<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques Marquette</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1675">1675</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques Marquette was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a>. In 1673 he and the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-jolliet">Louis Jolliet</a> travelled from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> as far as the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkansas">Arkansas</a> river, becoming the first Europeans to map much of its course. He founded a mission among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> at their Grand Village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaskaskia">Kaskaskia</a> and died on the shore of Lake Michigan in 1675.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-marquette.jpg" alt="Jacques Marquette" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wilhelm Lamprecht · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pere_Marquette_close-up.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
